 "verdict": "The marginal does NOT explain the split: 0.142 vs 0.087 top-1 for degenerate vs clean. Whatever makes a model collapse under two tokens is not simply that its prior is peaked. TV to the marginal at k=2 is 0.956 (degenerate) vs 0.960 (clean). NO RARE/COMMON EFFECT: the gap averages -0.036 with only 42% of cells positive. Short context and unfamiliar context are NOT the same thing here, so the CA result is about PROMPT LENGTH and must not be described as being about insufficient training data.",
 "_analysis_provenance": {
  "script": "evidence_falloff.py",
  "sha256": "95cf16c653aa9a9820e87141213d3ab6ccf313574e49b1273f76954573191f7f",
  "note": "sha256 of the analysis source at write time. If this does not match the file on disk, the analysis was produced by a different version of the code -- re-run it before reading the numbers (issue #38)."
 },
 "_note": "Asks whether the two-token collapse (F62-F69) is the extreme end of a general low-evidence retreat toward the model's prior, or a phenomenon of prompt length alone. Four measurements: the marginal p(x | BOS); the total-variation distance from the two-token conditional to it; an evidence ladder over real contexts of 1 to 32 tokens; and the same statistics split by context rarity, so 'short' is separated from 'unfamiliar' -- two things context length alone conflates. If rare contexts sit closer to the fallback at matched length, the CA finding generalises to data sparsity; if not, it is about prompt length only and must not be described otherwise. One forward pass per context, no generation and no ring dynamics."
}